 every day i'm on intensive care and i'll  probably be there for two to three weeks  you can see and you can think and feel  how long  that will affect me for for literally  for years  yeah it's a stark reminder isn't it and  and the age of patients that are in  intensive care what is the range  so the average age is 60 right  90 of our patients all had work  working before they came to us they had  normal lives so  the majority of patients i look after  are my age they  they look like me they look like my wife  they look like my friends  they have children the same age as i do  they are they are young people i'm 46 i  think i'm reasonably young  and fit and these are the patients i'm  looking after and this is  the same all around the country this is  what we are seeing  and are these people who does it include  people who had no underlying health  conditions  so that's a really great statement so i  have a really  obvious condition i've got  osteoarthritis with my knee that's an  underlying health condition so  yes includes people who have arthritis  maybe a broken finger  the term underlying heart condition a  health condition  is a made-up term that is meaningless to  our patients and meaningless to us it's  just  it doesn't mean anything everybody you  know the people within sky tv who have  underlying  health conditions okay i want to make it  very very clear from an intensive care  point of view we treat people without  prejudice and we do the best health care  we can to keep people's  quality of life whether they have an  underlying health condition is  irrelevant  um there are some articles in the papers  this morning and talking about  the decisions that need to be made by  people running intensive care units and  they're effectively deciding  uh who lives and who doesn't is is that  a reality in london hospitals  thank you that's a really really  important question and a really really  important point so  through the first wave of the pandemic  and now the legal  moral and ethical stance of intensive  care doctors  has not changed we treat people without  prejudice  and our priority is the patient in front  of us  okay and we are still there what we're  talking about  and that major incident signals is that  decision making capacity  may well be taken away from the  intensive care consultants  and been made because of resource  limitation  by politicians uh and other people who  make us  i would say that from an intensive care  point of view we  have not changed our legal moral and  ethical stance  which is to prioritize the patient in  front of us  right okay um i mean you're talking very  it's very clear talking to you  that resources are absolutely stretched  uh  to the very limit in terms of caring for  these patients in intensive care  what are the biggest challenges at the  moment the single biggest challenge  is staff we don't have enough  critical care nursing staff we don't  have enough doctors we have volunteers  coming in  we have very very senior doctors doctors  with no training with coming into work  both as junior doctors and to support  our nursing staff  it is amazing as i said before the level  of which people are willing to break  themselves to support the nhs  is awe-inspiring but it's not turning  out to be enough because you can't just  magic up a critical canvas it takes  years and years and years of training  you can't magic up a critical care  consultant it takes  years and years of training so it's  going to be staff it's not beds it's not  equipment  it's staff that's going to be the single  greatest bottleneck for us  i don't have a solution before you ask  me i don't know what the solution is  and that really really scares me  yeah i can i can hear it in your voice  and uh dr peter cherry um  the front line are supposed to be in the  priority group  for receiving the vaccine how many  intensivists  have been able to have the vaccine so i  can't speak  widely because the situation uh is  